{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.C1FuchsianExponents","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1FuchsianExponents","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Degree.Lemmas","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Derivative","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.C1NewtonRamification"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.both_ends_irrational","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.both_ends_irrational","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1FuchsianExponents.lean#L130-L138","doc":"**Both ends of C1 are quadratically irrational, for different reasons.**\nRecorded together because the *contrast* is the point: at `0` the weights come\nfrom an indicial equation and are computable by pure algebra; at `∞` they come\nfrom Stokes data and are not.  That the two ends need different arguments\n(multiplicity here, degree parity there) reflects that difference rather than\nbeing an accident of the proofs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.not_isSquare_indicialDiscriminant'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.not_isSquare_indicialDiscriminant'","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1FuchsianExponents.lean#L125-L128","doc":"Restated in Mathlib's `IsSquare` form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.not_isSquare_indicialDiscriminant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.not_isSquare_indicialDiscriminant","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1FuchsianExponents.lean#L102-L123","doc":"**C1's two weights at `0` are irrational over `ℚ(q)`.**  The indicial\ndiscriminant `q² − 6q + 5` is not a square in `ℚ[q]`, so the roots\n`t = (−(q−1) ± √(q²−6q+5))/2` of the indicial equation are not rational functions\nof `q`.\n\nProof by multiplicity, not degree parity (the degree is even, so parity is\nsilent): `f·f = p` forces `f(1)² = p(1) = 0`, hence `f(1) = 0`; differentiating,\n`p' = f'·f + f·f'`, so `p'(1) = 0`.  But `p'(1) = −4`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant_derivative_eval_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant_derivative_eval_one","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1FuchsianExponents.lean#L96-L100","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant_derivative","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant_derivative","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1FuchsianExponents.lean#L90-L94","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant_eval_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant_eval_one","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1FuchsianExponents.lean#L87-L88","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant_eq","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1FuchsianExponents.lean#L81-L85","doc":"The discriminant factors with **simple** roots `1` and `5`.  This is why the\ndegree-parity argument used on the `∞` side does not apply here: the degree is\neven. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.indicialDiscriminant","kind":"def","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1FuchsianExponents.lean#L77-L79","doc":"The discriminant of C1's indicial equation at the Fuchsian point `0`:\n`(q−1)² − 4(q−1) = q² − 6q + 5`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}